  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095012",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-106896",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b7f5608f-a9f5-5e6a-b308-540ed8b29259",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Value of an Unknown Given an Eigenvalue and Eigenvector of a Matrix",
      "problem": "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2k+1 & -k\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2k+1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =2k[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ k+1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2=2k, \\\\ k+1=2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2=2k\\,⟹\\,k=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k+1 & =2k \\\\ 1+1 & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2 & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=1.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
