  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095039",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-158440",
      "knowledge_point_id": "f59bd7df-afa9-5dcf-9dab-fc7f3cf291fa",
      "knowledge_point": "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF",
      "problem": "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{k},\\, & 0\\le x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4k}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{4k}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=4.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
