  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095152",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-152754",
      "knowledge_point_id": "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit",
      "problem": "${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t=$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{t}^{-1/2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
