  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095240",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-76910",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1889ba1a-09d4-5021-9ed2-3e531f8d5ef0",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve",
      "problem": "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,{t}^{3},\\,t+5⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=5.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{6}t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{6}t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1+3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =1+3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}(1+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =5+125-0 \\\\  & =130.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
