  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14095270",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-81678",
      "knowledge_point_id": "ee2920dc-93e1-5595-a751-62ad5e4f3035",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Velocity of a Particle Whose Acceleration Is Given By a Polynomial",
      "problem": "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2-3{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2-3{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2t-{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=4.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2(1)-(1)^{3}+C \\\\ 4 & =2-1+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=3+2t-{t}^{3}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
